WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:06.620
Video games are now the biggest entertainment
industry in the world.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:12.940
$47 billion dollars worth of video games were
sold worldwide in 2014 that's more than both

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.390
the music and film industries.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:21.630
And teenagers are now more addicted to video
game than ever before, a staggering 94 percent

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:27.780
of teenage girls and 99 percent of teenage
boys play video games on a regular basis.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.960
With so many modern games requiring fast reaction
times and lightning quick reflexes - does

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.120
playing video games actually have any real
and lasting effects on our reaction times

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.980
in day to day life?

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.970
Let's find out.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:52.690
Most contemporary video games such as Call
of Duty, Halo, Grand Theft Auto, and hundreds

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:58.050
more, require extremely fast processing of
sensory information to play.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:03.980
In this respect they push the limits of how
fast the human brain can process information

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.880
and make decisions based on that information.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.270
Video games force our brains to think and
respond faster than is needed in typical,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.270
everyday life.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:18.620
This is compounded by the fact that most video
games offer incentives for players to react

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:24.050
even faster to situations; in most games,
faster reactions lead to rewards such as increased

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.520
kills and faster lap times, to name but a
few examples.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.960
Now they say, practice makes perfect.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.180
And it's undeniably true.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.060
When we do anything in life, electrical impulses
are sent between the neurons in our brain.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.880
And the more we do that thing, the stronger
the link becomes between those neurons and

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.159
the faster they can communicate.

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.560
Which subsequently speeds up the rate at which
we perform the task.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.250
This is exactly how practice improves performance.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:58.210
Practice actually alters the physical structure
of our brain cells to quicken the flow of

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.610
information.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.190
So if that logic is true with everything else
we do in life, shouldn't it be the same for

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.190
video games.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:09.470
Should it not be the case that the more we
play fast paced video games the faster our

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:12.260
reaction times should be in everyday tasks.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:13.659
Well yes.

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.249
Just like with everything else we do, playing
video games does indeed strengthen the links

00:02:18.249 --> 00:02:24.849
between the neurons in our brains that are
related to processing and reacting to information.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:29.340
This was proved to be true by a recent study
by the department of Brain and Cognitive science

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.609
at the University of Rochester.

00:02:31.609 --> 00:02:37.889
The researchers took dozens of 18 to 25 year-olds
who where not ordinarily video game players;

00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:42.620
god knows how they found these peculiar teenagers
that don't play video games.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.560
They then split the subjects into two groups.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.090
One group played 50 hours of two particularly
fast-paced games, Unreal Tournament and Call

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.469
of Duty 2.

00:02:52.469 --> 00:02:58.450
The other group played 50 hours of the significantly
slower simulation game The Sims 2.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.329
Afterwards both teams were asked to complete
several tasks that were designed to test their

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:06.079
reaction and cognitive thinking skills.

00:03:06.079 --> 00:03:10.030
For example, the participants were asked to
look at a screen that showed a bunch of erratically

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:14.319
moving dots moving left and right across the
screen.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:19.349
The participants were tested on how quickly
they could answer a simple question.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:22.189
Are the dots moving left or right.

00:03:22.189 --> 00:03:27.220
They also underwent auditory reaction tests
to analyse how quickly both teams reacted

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.849
to sound as well as visual information.

00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:31.309
And the result?

00:03:31.309 --> 00:03:36.969
Well, the team that played the fast moving
action games were a whole 25 percent faster

00:03:36.969 --> 00:03:41.750
at answering the questions and completing
the tests than the other team who had spent

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.669
the morning building virtual houses with pretty
white picket fences.

00:03:45.669 --> 00:03:51.549
But the the action games didn't just improve
their reaction times, the researchers found

00:03:51.549 --> 00:03:56.459
that they also answered the questions more
accurately than the other team.

00:03:56.459 --> 00:04:01.200
The research team concluded that players of
fast paced video games would prove to be much

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:08.099
better at critical, real-life situations that
require fast reactions and quick thinking.

00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.370
Such as performing surgery or being on a battlefield.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.830
And I do mean a real battlefield, not one
made of pixels.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:19.570
Reacting to a situation is all about how fast
we can come to a decision about the challenge

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.290
we have been presented with.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:28.070
Our brain does this by rapidly gathering information
from our surroundings, both visual and auditory.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:32.700
It then puts this information together and
computes the best possible way to react.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:37.540
For example, when driving, if a car pulls
out in front of you from a junction without

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:38.540
warning.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.010
Your brain rapidly makes a decision based
on how fast you are traveling in relation

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:48.070
to the other car as well as other factors,
to determine whether or not you should slam

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:49.540
on the brakes.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.840
All this information is passed between neurons
in our brain, in a few milliseconds.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.510
However, people who regularly play video games
and have therefore strengthened the connections

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:05.320
between those neurons (those connections are
called axons by the way) would be able to

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:11.940
process this information significantly faster
and make the same decision in a fraction of

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.040
the time as a non-video game player.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.760
Potentially saving lives, in some situations.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.350
So when your parents ask you why you spend
so much time playing video games, simply tell

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:40.030
them you're practicing for a life or death
situation.

